<div class="sasha-practice-page">

  <div class="sasha-container">


    <!-- ============================================
         HEADER
    ============================================= -->

    <header class="sasha-practice-page-hero">

      <p class="sasha-practice-page-eyebrow">
        Sasha Best Practice
      </p>

      <h1>
        Pink Team Review
      </h1>

      <p class="sasha-practice-page-intro">
        Review the proposal storyboard, methodology, and
        work plan before substantial writing begins.
        A Pink Team Review tests whether the planned
        content is complete, logical, responsive,
        coordinated, strategically aligned, and ready
        to become proposal copy.
      </p>

    </header>


    <!-- ============================================
         WHAT IS PINK TEAM
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <h2>
          What is a Pink Team Review?
        </h2>

        <p>
          A Pink Team Review evaluates the planned proposal
          before the team has invested significant time
          writing polished prose.
        </p>

        <p>
          Depending on the pursuit, the review may examine
          the complete proposal storyboard or focus more
          narrowly on the methodology and work plan.
        </p>

        <p>
          The purpose is to determine whether the team's
          planned response is sufficiently developed to
          begin writing.
        </p>

        <div class="sasha-practice-callout">

          <strong>
            The central question
          </strong>

          <span>
            Does this plan give the writers enough complete,
            logical, responsive, and strategically aligned
            content to produce a strong proposal?
          </span>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
         WHY REVIEW NOW
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <h2>
          Why review before writing?
        </h2>

        <p>
          Pink Team happens while significant changes are
          still inexpensive.
        </p>

        <p>
          Rearranging a storyboard, adding a missing task,
          resolving an interface, or correcting the work
          breakdown structure is much easier before those
          ideas have become pages of finished proposal copy.
        </p>

        <p>
          This is the stage to question the logic of the
          planned response, not merely the wording.
        </p>

        <div class="sasha-practice-callout">

          <strong>
            Fix the plan before fixing the prose
          </strong>

          <span>
            Reorganizing a storyboard is inexpensive.
            Rewriting a completed methodology is not.
          </span>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
         REVIEWERS
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <h2>
          Choose useful reviewers
        </h2>

        <p>
          Pink Team reviewers should understand enough
          about project delivery to judge whether the
          proposed work plan makes sense.
        </p>

        <p>
          Experienced project managers and technical
          professionals can be especially valuable when
          reviewing methodology storyboards.
        </p>

        <div class="sasha-practice-role-grid">

          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Project managers
            </strong>

            <span>
              Test whether the proposed sequence,
              responsibilities, dependencies, and
              deliverables reflect realistic project
              delivery.
            </span>

          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Technical professionals
            </strong>

            <span>
              Test whether the activities and technical
              approach are complete and credible.
            </span>

          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Proposal reviewers
            </strong>

            <span>
              Test responsiveness, structure, evaluator
              clarity, and alignment with the RFP.
            </span>

          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Sasha
            </strong>

            <span>
              Compares the storyboard against the RFP,
              scope, win strategy, personnel, project
              evidence, lessons learned, deliverables,
              and schedule assumptions.
            </span>

          </div>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
         MATERIALS
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <h2>
          Prepare the review material
        </h2>

        <p>
          Reviewers should see the planning material in
          enough context to understand what the team is
          trying to accomplish.
        </p>

        <div class="sasha-practice-check-grid">

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>RFP and relevant addenda</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Scope of work</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Evaluation criteria</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Current win strategy</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Proposal storyboard</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Methodology or work plan</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Proposed personnel</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Important project issues</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Critical success factors</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Known schedule assumptions</span>
          </div>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
         REVIEW STEPS
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-page-heading-row">

        <h2>
          Conduct the Pink Team Review
        </h2>

      </div>


      <div class="sasha-practice-step-list">


        <!-- STEP 1 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            01
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Confirm every scope item has a response
            </h3>

            <p>
              Compare the storyboard or work plan directly
              against the RFP scope.
            </p>

            <p>
              Every required service, phase, deliverable,
              milestone, decision point, and client
              responsibility should have a clear home.
            </p>

            <div class="sasha-practice-callout">

              <strong>
                Watch for acknowledged scope
              </strong>

              <span>
                A scope item has not been addressed merely
                because the storyboard mentions it.
                It must be turned into actionable work.
              </span>

            </div>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 2 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            02
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Test the task structure
            </h3>

            <p>
              Review whether the proposed tasks form a
              sensible work breakdown structure.
            </p>

            <ul>

              <li>
                Are the tasks logical?
              </li>

              <li>
                Are important activities missing?
              </li>

              <li>
                Is anything duplicated unnecessarily?
              </li>

              <li>
                Does the structure reflect how the project
                will actually be delivered?
              </li>

            </ul>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 3 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            03
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Check the objectives
            </h3>

            <p>
              Each major task should have a clear reason
              for existing.
            </p>

            <p>
              Reviewers should be able to distinguish
              between:
            </p>

            <ul>

              <li>
                the objective the task must accomplish;
              </li>

              <li>
                the activities the team will perform; and
              </li>

              <li>
                the deliverable that results.
              </li>

            </ul>

            <p>
              If these ideas are blurred together, the work
              plan may not be sufficiently developed.
            </p>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 4 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            04
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Test the activities
            </h3>

            <p>
              Determine whether the planned activities are
              sufficient to accomplish the stated objectives.
            </p>

            <p>
              Ask:
            </p>

            <ul>

              <li>
                What will the team actually do?
              </li>

              <li>
                Is the technical approach credible?
              </li>

              <li>
                Are important reviews, decisions, or
                investigations missing?
              </li>

              <li>
                Does the activity respond to the specific
                conditions of this project?
              </li>

            </ul>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 5 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            05
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Confirm the deliverables
            </h3>

            <p>
              Review whether each major task produces a
              clear result.
            </p>

            <p>
              Deliverables may include:
            </p>

            <ul>

              <li>
                reports;
              </li>

              <li>
                designs;
              </li>

              <li>
                drawings;
              </li>

              <li>
                recommendations;
              </li>

              <li>
                approvals;
              </li>

              <li>
                decisions;
              </li>

              <li>
                models;
              </li>

              <li>
                schedules; or
              </li>

              <li>
                other tangible project outputs.
              </li>

            </ul>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 6 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            06
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Review dependencies and sequence
            </h3>

            <p>
              Projects are not collections of independent
              tasks.
            </p>

            <p>
              Review where:
            </p>

            <ul>

              <li>
                one task produces information needed by
                another;
              </li>

              <li>
                decisions control downstream work;
              </li>

              <li>
                approvals affect later phases;
              </li>

              <li>
                tasks can occur concurrently; and
              </li>

              <li>
                one activity cannot begin until another is
                complete.
              </li>

            </ul>

            <p>
              The overall sequence should make sense.
            </p>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 7 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            07
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Make multidisciplinary interfaces visible
            </h3>

            <p>
              Review where disciplines, specialists, and
              organizations need to coordinate.
            </p>

            <p>
              Look for:
            </p>

            <ul>

              <li>
                shared inputs;
              </li>

              <li>
                design interfaces;
              </li>

              <li>
                interdisciplinary reviews;
              </li>

              <li>
                handoffs;
              </li>

              <li>
                potential conflicts; and
              </li>

              <li>
                integrated deliverables.
              </li>

            </ul>

            <div class="sasha-practice-callout">

              <strong>
                One project
              </strong>

              <span>
                The methodology should describe one
                coordinated project, not several disciplines
                working independently beside one another.
              </span>

            </div>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 8 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            08
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Confirm client responsibilities
            </h3>

            <p>
              Identify where the client must provide
              information, participate in reviews, make
              decisions, approve work, provide access, or
              confirm assumptions.
            </p>

            <p>
              Make sure those responsibilities are realistic
              and clearly connected to the work plan.
            </p>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 9 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            09
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Test the work plan against project risks
            </h3>

            <p>
              Important risks should affect the methodology.
            </p>

            <p>
              If the strategy claims that the team will
              manage a major risk, reviewers should be able
              to identify the task or activity where that
              risk is actually addressed.
            </p>

            <div class="sasha-practice-callout">

              <strong>
                Red flag
              </strong>

              <span>
                If the methodology says a risk is important
                but contains no work that manages it, flag
                the inconsistency.
              </span>

            </div>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 10 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            10
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Confirm the right people are involved
            </h3>

            <p>
              Compare the work plan with the proposed team.
            </p>

            <p>
              Review whether:
            </p>

            <ul>

              <li>
                each important role is covered;
              </li>

              <li>
                proposed specialists appear where their
                expertise is needed;
              </li>

              <li>
                responsibilities are understandable; and
              </li>

              <li>
                the storyboard depends on anyone who is not
                actually part of the proposed team.
              </li>

            </ul>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 11 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            11
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Test alignment with the win strategy
            </h3>

            <p>
              The methodology should make the win strategy
              operational.
            </p>

            <p>
              Ask:
            </p>

            <ul>

              <li>
                Does the approach address the key project
                issues?
              </li>

              <li>
                Does it support the critical success
                factors?
              </li>

              <li>
                Are relevant strengths visible in the work
                plan?
              </li>

              <li>
                Are differentiators reflected in how the
                team will perform the work?
              </li>

              <li>
                Is relevant experience informing the
                approach?
              </li>

            </ul>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 12 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            12
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Decide whether the plan is ready to write
            </h3>

            <p>
              At the end of the review, determine whether
              there is enough complete and coordinated
              substance to begin drafting.
            </p>

            <p>
              Do not force completeness when information is
              genuinely unavailable.
            </p>

            <p>
              Record important gaps, assign actions, and
              resolve them where necessary before the
              proposal team spends significant time
              producing finished prose.
            </p>

          </div>

        </div>


      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
         SASHA ROLE
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-value-panel">

        <div>

          <h2>
            Sasha's Pink Team contribution
          </h2>

        </div>

        <div class="sasha-practice-value-copy">

          <p>
            Sasha can compare the storyboard directly
            against the RFP scope and identify omissions,
            mismatches, or requirements that have not been
            turned into actionable work.
          </p>

          <p>
            She can also compare the work plan against the
            project's issues, critical success factors,
            proposed personnel, relevant lessons learned
            from Phil, schedule assumptions, deliverables,
            and win strategy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sasha should flag inconsistencies rather than
            quietly repairing them.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the methodology relies on a specialist who
            is not on the proposed team, if an important
            risk has no methodology response, or if a scope
            item has been acknowledged but not addressed,
            Sasha should make that visible to the team.
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
         FINDINGS
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <h2>
          Record the Pink Team findings
        </h2>

        <p>
          Capture the issues that must be resolved before
          writing proceeds.
        </p>

        <div class="sasha-practice-check-grid">

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Missing scope items</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Missing or weak activities</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Unclear objectives</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Undefined deliverables</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Unclear dependencies</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Multidisciplinary gaps</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Unclear client responsibilities</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Risks without methodology responses</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Personnel gaps</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Win strategy misalignment</span>
          </div>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
         FINAL CHECK
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <h2>
          Is the storyboard ready?
        </h2>

        <div class="sasha-practice-check-grid">

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              Every RFP scope item has a clear response.
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              The tasks form a logical work plan.
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              Objectives, activities, and deliverables are clear.
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              Important dependencies are visible.
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              Multidisciplinary interfaces are addressed.
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              Client responsibilities are understood.
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              Important risks affect the methodology.
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              The right people are included.
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              The work plan supports the win strategy.
            </span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>
              There is enough substance to begin writing.
            </span>
          </div>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
         RETURN
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <a
          href="/plan{{#if pursuitId}}?pursuit={{pursuitId}}{{/if}}"
        >
          Return to Plan
        </a>

      </div>

    </section>


  </div>

</div>